Sval: a content-classifier feature that is off drops its engine and stales its builds

ContentClassifierService::ProcessListChanges skipped a feature that the
engines prefs leave out, so lists that changed while it was off marked
nothing out of date. Turning it straight back on found its old engine
still installed and did not rebuild it, and a build scheduled just
before it went off, for the lists Off wrote, then installed over it:
Sval's ads stopped blocking until some other list changed. The fix: a feature
that is off drops its engine and bumps its version, so turning it on
builds from the lists it names then and an older build goes stale.

PruneInactiveEngines also erased an off feature's version. The next
version then restarted at one, equal to the version a build scheduled
before the feature went off had captured, so that build counted as
current. The fix keeps versions past the engines they guard.

diff --git a/toolkit/components/content-classifier/ContentClassifierService.cpp b/toolkit/components/content-classifier/ContentClassifierService.cpp
index 900c3a7178..35fee5427e 100644
--- a/toolkit/components/content-classifier/ContentClassifierService.cpp
+++ b/toolkit/components/content-classifier/ContentClassifierService.cpp
@@ -1301,11 +1301,9 @@ void ContentClassifierService::PruneInactiveEngines(
       iter.Remove();
     }
   }
-  for (auto iter = mFeatureVersions.Iter(); !iter.Done(); iter.Next()) {
-    if (!activeFeatureNames.Contains(iter.Key())) {
-      iter.Remove();
-    }
-  }
+  // Sval: a feature's version outlives its engine. A version that restarted
+  // when the feature turned off would let a build scheduled before that
+  // install over one scheduled after it turned back on.
 }
 
 void ContentClassifierService::ProcessListChanges(
@@ -1323,6 +1321,12 @@ void ContentClassifierService::ProcessListChanges(
     MutexAutoLock lock(mLock);
     for (const auto& feature : GetFeatures()) {
       if (!activeNames.Contains(nsCString(feature.mName))) {
+        // Sval: a feature that is off keeps no engine, and a build under way
+        // for it goes stale, so turning it on builds from the lists it names
+        // then. Its lists can change while it is off, and nothing else marks
+        // the engine it had as out of date.
+        mEngines.Remove(nsCString(feature.mName));
+        ++mFeatureVersions.LookupOrInsert(feature.mName);
         continue;
       }
       bool affected = &feature == aRebuild ||
